-- title:
--   Box expressions on space-time and their formal partial derivatives
-- statement:
--   This file introduces a small symbolic calculus for real-valued functions on space-time $\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}^3$. A space-time point is written $z=(t,x)$ with $x=(x_0,x_1,x_2)$, and its four coordinates are numbered $z_0=t$, $z_1=x_0$, $z_2=x_1$, $z_3=x_2$.
--
--   The building blocks are one-variable *jet expressions* (introduced in an earlier file): formal expressions $e$ generated by rational constants, the identity $s\mapsto s$, sums, products, rational affine substitutions $s\mapsto e(as+b)$ with $a,b\in\mathbb{Q}$, the flat functions $F_p(s)=p(1/s)\,e^{-1/s}$ for $s>0$ and $F_p(s)=0$ for $s\le 0$ (with $p\in\mathbb{Q}[X]$), and the function $R(s)=1/\bigl(\psi(s)+\psi(1-s)\bigr)$, where $\psi(s)=e^{-1/s}$ for $s>0$ and $\psi(s)=0$ for $s\le 0$. Each jet expression $e$ has a value $e(s)\in\mathbb{R}$ and a formal derivative $e'$, again a jet expression, with $\frac{d}{ds}e(s)=e'(s)$.
--
--   1. **Coordinate functionals.** For $j\in\{0,1,2,3\}$, $\pi_j:\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}^3\to\mathbb{R}$ is the continuous linear map $\pi_j(z)=z_j$. On the space-time basis $\mathbf e_0=(1,0)$, $\mathbf e_{k+1}=(0,e_k)$ (where $e_0,e_1,e_2$ is the standard basis of $\mathbb{R}^3$) one has $\pi_i(\mathbf e_j)=\delta_{ij}$.
--   2. **Box expressions.** The formal expressions $E$ generated by rational constants $q$, coordinate terms $e[z_j]$ (a jet expression $e$ applied to the single coordinate $z_j$), sums $E+F$ and products $EF$. Each $E$ evaluates to a function on space-time:
--   $$q(z)=q,\qquad e[z_j](z)=e\bigl(\pi_j(z)\bigr),\qquad (E+F)(z)=E(z)+F(z),\qquad (EF)(z)=E(z)\,F(z).$$
--   3. **Formal partial derivatives.** For $j\in\{0,1,2,3\}$, $\partial_jE$ is the box expression defined recursively by $\partial_jq=0$; $\partial_j\,e[z_i]=e'[z_i]$ if $i=j$ and $\partial_j\,e[z_i]=0$ if $i\neq j$; $\partial_j(E+F)=\partial_jE+\partial_jF$; and $\partial_j(EF)=(\partial_jE)F+E(\partial_jF)$.
--   4. **Mixed formal derivatives.** For a finite list $\alpha=(j_1,\dots,j_k)$ of coordinate indices, $\partial^{\alpha}E=\partial_{j_1}\bigl(\partial_{j_2}(\cdots\partial_{j_k}E)\bigr)$; that is, $\partial^{()}E=E$ and $\partial^{(j,\alpha)}E=\partial_j(\partial^{\alpha}E)$, so the last index of the list acts first.
--   5. **Expression fields.** A triple $(E_0,E_1,E_2)$ of box expressions defines the time-dependent vector field $u(t,x)=\bigl(E_0(t,x),E_1(t,x),E_2(t,x)\bigr)\in\mathbb{R}^3$.
--   6. **Closed period cell.** $K=[0,1]\times[0,10]^3\subset\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}^3$, one unit time period times the closed spatial period cell.
--
--   Box expressions are explicit closed formulas whose mixed partial derivatives of every order are again box expressions, produced by a purely syntactic recursion that mirrors the sum and product rules. They are used to describe a velocity or forcing field exactly on one space-time period $K$, which is the first step towards showing that such a field and all of its derivatives can be evaluated by an algorithm.
--
--   **Formalization Note** `BoxExpr` is an inductive type with constructors `const`, `coord`, `add`, `mul`; the objects above are `coordinateLinear`, `BoxExpr.eval`, `BoxExpr.diff`, `BoxExpr.mixed`, `expressionField` and `closedPeriodCell` (the order interval `Set.Icc (0,0) (1, fun _ => 10)` in the product order). The simp lemmas `coordinateLinear_eq` and `coordinateLinear_basis` identify $\pi_j$ with the coordinate vector `flatten z` and evaluate it on `spacetimeBasis`. Jet expressions are the type `JetExpr`, with evaluation `JetExpr.eval` and formal derivative `JetExpr.diff`.
